// Konstelacja Motywów — dane (filmy z oceną osobistą 7+, źródło: Notion Movies)
// Motywy wg pojęć z literatury; def to czysty opis teorii (bez filmów), WHY opisuje konkretne filmy
const MOTIFS = {
  wound:   { pl:"Trauma dziedziczona", src:"Bowen, Fraiberg — „Ghosts in the nursery“", en:"Intergenerational trauma",
             def:"Psychological harm transmitted from parents to children without ever being spoken — through repetition, expectation, and the anxious ways a family organizes its love. Bowen’s family systems theory treats the individual as a node in an emotional field spanning generations: what one generation cannot process, the next inherits as temperament, symptom, or fate. Fraiberg named the mechanism ghosts in the nursery — the parents’ repressed past visiting the child’s cradle. The wound rarely announces itself; it disguises as character, as family rules, as “how we do things here.” It heals only when someone traces the pattern back to its origin and feels what the original bearer could not.", c:"#d95d76" },
  shadow:  { pl:"Cień", src:"Jung — archetyp cienia", en:"The Shadow (Jung)",
             def:"Jung’s term for the personality’s disowned contents — instincts, desires, capacities and shames unacceptable to the conscious self, exiled to the unconscious but never gone. The shadow is not simply evil: it also holds the vitality and truth a person was forced to renounce. It operates by projection — we meet it in others, in enemies, in fascination that feels like contempt. Cut off from awareness, it rules from within: the refused returns as mood, accident, compulsion, fate. Integration — acknowledging the shadow rather than fighting it — is the first labor of becoming whole. Until then, whoever refuses to face the shadow lives inside it.", c:"#a86bb7" },
  mask:    { pl:"Persona", src:"Jung — persona, maska społeczna", en:"The Persona (Jung)",
             def:"The mask every person assembles to meet the social world: role, title, style — the negotiated face. Jung considered it necessary: without a persona there is no interface between self and society. The pathology begins when the mask is mistaken for the face — when the role starts making the person’s decisions. A persona that succeeds too well enslaves its wearer: the world rewards the mask, the mask thickens, and what is underneath atrophies from disuse. In artists and performers the persona doubles as an instrument — armour, weapon, laboratory. Its power and its danger are one: to perform a self convincingly is to discover how movable the self always was.", c:"#e2b458" },
  veil:    { pl:"Symulakr", src:"Baudrillard — symulakry i hiperrzeczywistość", en:"Simulacrum (Baudrillard)",
             def:"Baudrillard’s copy for which no original exists. A culture of signs stops referring to reality and begins referring only to other signs, until the map precedes the territory. The result is hyperreality — experiences engineered to seem more real than the real, until the difference can no longer be checked. The hallmark is the loss of a reference point: no outside from which to verify the inside. Dreams, simulations, media and memory become equally valid textures of world. The disturbing part is not the fakeness but the remainder: inside a perfected simulation, feelings stay stubbornly real — with nowhere legitimate to happen.", c:"#7fbfa4" },
  time:    { pl:"Pamięć i trwanie", src:"Bergson — durée; Proust — pamięć mimowolna", en:"Duration & memory (Bergson)",
             def:"Bergson’s durée: time as it is actually lived — a continuous flow in which past and present interpenetrate, as opposed to clock-time, the spatialized sequence that clocks and calendars measure. The psyche is duration: each moment carries all previous moments, swelling like a snowball. Proust mapped its mechanism — involuntary memory, where a taste or a paving-stone suddenly returns the past entire, unmediated and unsummoned. Against this stands irreversibility: durée flows one way only. Loss is then not an event but the medium of life itself; nostalgia is the mind’s attempt to hold what duration by definition cannot keep.", c:"#d3825e" },
  crossing:{ pl:"Bycie-ku-śmierci", src:"Heidegger — Sein zum Tode", en:"Being-toward-death (Heidegger)",
             def:"Heidegger: human existence is finite, and authenticity begins where one stops fleeing that finitude. Being-toward-death is not morbid preoccupation but lucidity — death as the ownmost possibility, non-relational, unsurpassable: the horizon against which a life acquires shape and urgency. Most of culture, Heidegger argues, is evasion: the anonymous “one” murmurs “one dies,” as if it concerned no one in particular. To own one’s mortality is to be thrown back on choice: with no unlimited time, every yes becomes a real yes. Ghosts, farewells and afterlives are the imagination’s training ground for exactly this.", c:"#8f8fd8" },
  cosmos:  { pl:"Wzniosłość", src:"Burke, Kant — the sublime", en:"The Sublime (Kant)",
             def:"Burke and Kant: the experience of what overwhelms the senses and the mind’s power to comprehend — vastness, power, infinity, darkness. Unlike the beautiful, which charms because it fits our measure, the sublime first terrifies and annihilates, then exalts: reason discovers in itself a superiority to everything the senses cannot contain. Kant distinguished the mathematically sublime (the number too large to number, the starry sky) from the dynamically sublime (the storm, the volcano). It is the meeting point of terror and awe, where the self feels annihilated and enlarged in the same gesture — the aesthetic experience closest to religion without being one.", c:"#eec384" },
  living:  { pl:"Anima mundi", src:"Platon, Jung — dusza świata", en:"Anima mundi",
             def:"The world-soul: the ancient thesis, from Plato’s Timaeus through the alchemists to Jung, that the cosmos is not dead mechanism but a single living being, matter itself ensouled and every part animate. Against the modern view of nature as stockpile and resource, anima mundi restores interiority to the world: places, animals and things have perspective, presence, intent. Jung revived it as a psychological necessity — unless the world has a soul, the human soul has no home. It is the metaphysics of relationship: knowing is meeting, and nature is not scenery but the larger body to which every body belongs.", c:"#aebd7e" },
  mastery: { pl:"Sublimacja", src:"Freud — sublimacja popędów w sztukę", en:"Sublimation (Freud)",
             def:"Freud: the fate of the drives — desire redirected from its original aim toward socially valued creation, the artist’s libido becoming the work. It is the one defence mechanism he admired: not repression, which buries and warps, but transformation — heat becoming light. Its economy is exact: the intensity that would have been a life becomes a thing that outlives the life. Hence its shadow side: the work consumes the maker, technique perfects itself by hollowing out the person, and the masterpiece tends to bill its author at full price. Mastery, psychologically read, is a love affair with an object that cannot refuse.", c:"#af7d4e" },
  bonds:   { pl:"Communitas", src:"Victor Turner — wspólnota liminalna", en:"Communitas (Turner)",
             def:"Victor Turner, from ritual studies: the intense, egalitarian fellowship that appears in liminality — thresholds where social structure dissolves. In rites of passage, initiands are stripped of status and made equal in vulnerability; what arises between them is not hierarchy but spontaneous, direct, total belonging. Communitas cannot be institutionalized — attempts to schedule it produce structure again. It appears in shared ordeal, pilgrimage, marginality, chosen family: bonds made not by blood or contract but by going through something together. It is society’s counterweight — where structure divides and ranks, the threshold briefly remembers that people are simply people.", c:"#f2e6c8" },
  flesh:   { pl:"Abiekcja", src:"Kristeva — „Powers of Horror“", en:"The Abject (Kristeva)",
             def:"Kristeva: that which disturbs identity, system and order — what does not respect borders, positions, rules. Its exhibit is the body’s reality: blood, milk, wounds, meat, the corpse, the fluids that pass from inside to out. The abject is not simply filth: it is what each person must permanently beat away in order to be “I” — and it comes from one’s own body, so the border it violates is the self. Hence the peculiar response: horror and fascination at once, revulsion that reveals dependence. To face the abject is to face that the self is a provisional agreement over a piece of thinking matter — and the agreement can lapse at any wound.", c:"#e0a898" },
  rite:    { pl:"Sacrum i profanum", src:"Eliade — hierofanie, mit wiecznego powrotu", en:"Sacred & profane (Eliade)",
             def:"Eliade’s two modes of experiencing the world. Profane space is homogeneous and neutral — geography, calendar, utility. Sacred space appears when a hierophany occurs: the sacred manifests itself, splits the world into centre and periphery, and a place becomes qualitatively different — a temple, a grove, a threshold. In sacred time, ritual re-enacts the myth of creation: the eternal return, by which repeating the archetypal gesture lifts the participant out of historical time toward origin. Ritual is therefore not symbolism but operation — it works, mechanically, on the world and the person. The rules are absolute because the stakes are cosmic: the wrong word at the right hour opens something, or closes it.", c:"#bf94d8" },
  madness: { pl:"Rozdwojenie jaźni", src:"Laing — „The Divided Self“", en:"The divided self (Laing)",
             def:"R.D. Laing, anti-psychiatry: psychosis is not hardware failure but a comprehensible strategy — the self splitting in order to survive an unbearable situation. The divided self separates a false self (presented to the world, compliant) from the buried true self, which risks annihilation if exposed. The split protects, but at the price of a life lived by proxy: the person watches their own existence happen to them. Doubling, dissociation, the double who knows too much — not decorative motifs but accurate phenomenology. Laing’s demand: the mad person is making sense, if one listens for the situation their madness is answering.", c:"#c94b56" },
  abyss:   { pl:"Panoptikon", src:"Foucault — nadzór i dyscyplina", en:"Panopticon (Foucault)",
             def:"From Bentham’s prison design, theorized by Foucault: a machine in which the mere possibility of being watched at any moment makes inmates internalize surveillance and discipline themselves. Its modern form is not architecture but normalization — institutions, markets, media and metrics producing docile, useful bodies without any tyrant. Power here is not held; it circulates. Cruelty is procedural, filed in forms. The critical insight: the system runs on normals — ordinary people executing roles, each step small enough to feel like nothing. The horror is not a monster with a face but a process with a filing system, and everyone inside is simultaneously guard and guarded.", c:"#a39885" },
  feminist:{ pl:"Druga płeć", src:"Beauvoir — kobieta jako Inna", en:"The Second Sex (Beauvoir)",
             def:"Beauvoir’s thesis: one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman. Woman is constructed as the Other — humanity defaulting to man, woman defined relationally, as deviation, mystery, nature against culture. The making happens through upbringing, myth, economy and law, so “femininity” is neither essence nor fate but a manufactured situation. Exposing this making is the critical act: what was presented as natural turns out to be authored, and what is authored can be rewritten. The relation of Self and Other structures everything downstream — desire, motherhood, diagnoses of hysteria, whose gaze counts as knowledge. To unmake it is not to invert the hierarchy but to reclaim the right of self-definition.", c:"#d16a9f" },
  esoterica:{ pl:"Inicjacja", src:"Eliade — próby wtajemniczenia", en:"Initiation (Eliade)",
             def:"Eliade: initiation is the archetypal pattern of all becoming — the rites of passage that convert a person from one state of being to another. Its structure is triadic: separation (death of the old identity), liminal ordeal (the trial that cannot be shortcut), reincorporation (rebirth under a new name). Initiation is not information but transformation: the hidden knowledge cannot be told, only entered — the threshold does not open for curiosity, only for stakes. Its law is exchange: the door costs exactly what the initiate cannot comfortably spare. Every serious art, therapy, love and grief repeats the pattern; the esoteric traditions simply kept the blueprint.", c:"#dda04a" },
  philos:  { pl:"Egzystencja", src:"Sartre — egzystencja poprzedza esencję", en:"Existence (Sartre)",
             def:"Sartre’s formula: existence precedes essence. There is no given human nature, no pre-assigned meaning, no divine script — a person first exists, thrown into the world, and only afterwards defines what they are through choices. This is freedom, and freedom is vertigo: without guarantees, every choice is total commitment and total responsibility, including responsibility for what one becomes. Bad faith is the escape — pretending to be a fixed thing (waiter, victim, official) with no say in the matter. The question “how should one live?” loses its external answer and becomes a task: to make, of a finite and unplanned life, something that could have been only yours.", c:"#d6c67f" },
};

